  1. What is method overloading?
  example - Developing a calculator. You want to add two integers, two decimal numbers,
  or three integers.

  You could create three different methods:
  - addTwoIntegers()
  - addTwoDoubles()
  - addThreeIntegers()
  But this makes the code harder to remember and use.

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    How does method overloading work?

    public static void main(String[] args) {
            Calculator calc = new Calculator();

            System.out.println(calc.add(10, 20));
            System.out.println(calc.add(2.5, 3.5));
            System.out.println(calc.add(10, 20, 30));
        }

    What are the ways to overload a method?
    There are three common ways to overload a method.

        A.  By changing the number of parameters
            void show(int a) {
                    System.out.println(a);
                }

                void show(int a, int b) {
                    System.out.println(a + ", " + b);
                }

        B. By changing the data types of parameters
            void show(int value) {
                    System.out.println("Integer: " + value);
                }

                void show(String value) {
                    System.out.println("String: " + value);
                }

                public static void main(String[] args) {
                    Display d = new Display();

                    d.show(100);
                    d.show("Java");
                }

        C. By changing the order of parameter types
            void show(int a, String b) {
                    System.out.println(a + " " + b);
                }

                void show(String a, int b) {
                    System.out.println(a + " " + b);
                }

                public static void main(String[] args) {
                    Display d = new Display();

                    d.show(10, "Java");
                    d.show("Java", 10);
                }

